  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients pay for people who solve a specific problem well. They judge you by evidence of one strong skill, not by a list of interests.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is choosing one trial and finishing it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and choosing with many interests: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools suggest careers from interests and summarise roles. Treat the output as a list to test, not an answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Self-knowledge, real trials and decisions you own hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to generate options; later, test them with people and small projects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general choosing with many interests path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Career test</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Quick list</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Limited depth</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Talking to professionals</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Honest view</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs outreach</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Short project</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Real feedback</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Takes weeks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn choosing with many interests cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Choosing with many interests is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free self-assessment worksheets.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube day-in-the-life videos.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM taster lessons.</li>
    <li>A teacher or counsellor who knows you.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    This week, list your interests, cluster them and pick one two-week trial.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cluster">Cluster your interests into three groups</h2>

  <p>
    Write every interest you have on separate lines, then group them by what you actually do in each.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Group</th>
          <th>What you do</th>
          <th>Example interests</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Making</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What you do">Build, write, draw or design something</td>
          <td data-label="Example interests">Coding, writing, art, cooking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Understanding</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What you do">Study, analyse or explain</td>
          <td data-label="Example interests">Science, history, data, psychology</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Helping</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What you do">Work with and support people</td>
          <td data-label="Example interests">Teaching, health, counselling, social work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Organising</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What you do">Plan, manage or sell</td>
          <td data-label="Example interests">Events, business, operations</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Most people find two groups pull harder than the rest. Start there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trials">Run small trials before you decide</h2>

  <p>
    A two-week trial tells you more than months of thinking.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Option</th>
          <th>Two-week trial</th>
          <th>What to notice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
